### Step 2 – Use slicers (single-select, multi-select)

#### What I did in Power BI
- Created new page: `3.1.2 – Slicers` and copied a few visuals (column chart, bar chart, table).
- Added a **Year slicer**:
  - Field: `dim_date[Year]`
  - Slicer type: Dropdown
  - Single select = On (user can only pick one year)
- Added a **Customer slicer**:
  - Field: `dim_customer[Customer Label]`
  - Vertical list with search box enabled
  - Multi-select allowed (CTRL to select multiple customers)
- Added a **Product Category slicer**:
  - Field: `dim_product[Category]`
  - Horizontal slicer for quick selection of categories
  - Multi-select allowed
- (Optional) Added a **Region slicer**:
  - Field: `dim_store[Region]`
  - Used as dropdown, depending on whether I want single- or multi-select.
- Tested all slicers to see how they change:
  - Column chart (Sales by Month)
  - Bar chart (Sales by Region)
  - Table (Top customers)

#### What I learned
- Slicers filter all visuals on the page by changing the filter context.
- **Single-select slicers** (e.g. Year) are useful when users should focus on exactly one value.
- **Multi-select slicers** are useful for comparing multiple values (e.g. several customers or product categories).
- Search within a slicer helps when a column has many values (e.g. customer names).
- Slicers are different from visual-/page-level filters because:
  - Users see them directly on the report canvas.
  - They let users interactively change the analysis.

