### Step 3 – Use filters (visual, page, report)

#### What I did in Power BI
- Created new page: `3.1.3 – Filters` and copied existing visuals (Sales by Month, Sales by Region, Customer table).
- Used a **visual-level filter**:
  - On the Region bar/column chart, filtered `dim_product[Category]` to only show one category (e.g. Bikes).
- Used a **page-level filter**:
  - Added `dim_date[Year]` to "Filters on this page" and selected a single year (e.g. 2020).
  - All visuals on this page now only show that year.
- Used a **report-level filter**:
  - Added `dim_store[Region]` to "Filters on all pages" and selected one Region.
  - All pages in the report were filtered to that Region.
- Created a **Top N filter** on the customer table:
  - Filter type: Top N
  - Top 10 by `[Total Sales]`
  - Table now shows only Top 10 customers.
- Filtered on a **measure**:
  - Added `[Total Sales]` to Filters on this visual.
  - Only showed rows where `[Total Sales]` is greater than a chosen threshold.

#### What I learned
- **Visual-level filters** affect only one visual.
- **Page-level filters** affect all visuals on that page.
- **Report-level filters** affect all pages in the report (use with care).
- **Top N filters** are useful to focus on best-performing items (e.g. Top 10 customers).
- Measures like `[Total Sales]` can be used in filters to show only high-value results (e.g. Sales > 10.000).

